  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAn area of low pressure is currently located over the Ohio\nValley. Widespread clouds have already overspread the region and\nmoisture is increasing. Some light rain will be developing by\naround daybreak and a steady rain will be in place over much of\nthe region by the mid to late morning hours. With isentropic\nlift in place, steady light to moderate rainfall is expected\nthrough the day with the heaviest totals across the Catskills.\nTemperatures will be steady during the morning hours (ranging\nfrom the mid 30s over the Adirondacks to the mid 40s elsewhere) \nbut will be falling during the afternoon hours. Dynamic cooling\nand wet-bulbing will allow precip to start to change over to\nsnow by late today over the Adirondacks, with a dusting across\nthe highest terrain by this evening.\n\nThe area of low pressure will be passing by to the south across\nthe northern mid Atlantic States for tonight and will be\nreaching the NYC Metro area by Friday morning. As this occurs,\nthe low-level flow out of the north will be allowing some colder\ntemps to filter in at low levels. Temps aloft will continue to\nslowly cool as well, as the large upper level trough to the west\nmoves closer to the area. With steady precip still ongoing, more\nand more of the high terrain will be changing over from rain to\nsnow, including the eastern Catskills and southern Greens of\nsouthern VT. Valley areas will be falling into the 30s as well. \n\nOn Friday morning, the low pressure area will be passing close\nto Long Island and southern New England. Deformation on the\nnorthwest side of the storm will continue to allow for some\nsteady precip into the morning hours and some mesoscale models\nsuggests that some banding of precip is possible as well. As\nthis occurs, even valley areas may briefly change over to snow,\nalthough any accumulation will generally be light (about an inch\nor less) and mainly limited to colder surfaces, like grassy\nareas. Mesoscale models, including the new RRFS and 3km HRRR, as\nsuggested this possibility, but have had little run to run\nconsistency on where any banding may be, the exact timing and \nsnowfall amounts. \n\nThere remains some uncertainty in the guidance, such as how long\nprecip lingers through the day on Friday, where any banded\nprecip may be and just how much snowfall occurs for the high\nterrain areas. As of now, NBM probabilities suggests that only\nsmall parts of the highest terrain of the Adirondacks, Catskills\nand Greens may exceed 4\" of snow, as there is a 40-70% chance\nfor some the highest peaks. NBM suggest that most high terrain\nareas look to top out in the 1-3\" range. Valley areas have a\nfairly low probability to exceed 1\", with most valley areas\nunder a 25% chance to get over an inch at this time. WSSI shows\nthe most likely category for the highest terrain is \"Minor \nImpacts\" at this time, with slippery travel being the main\nimpact. Will continue the Winter Weather Advisory for the\neastern Catskills, southern Adirondacks and southern Greens into\nFriday morning, with slippery travel being the main threat at\nthis time. \n\nPrecip should be tapering off to snow showers and flurries by\nlate Friday. Some additional flurries are possible Friday night\ninto Saturday with the upper level trough still overhead, but no\nadditional accumulation is expected. Some lake effect is also\npossible by later in the weekend, but this looks primarily west\nof the region thanks to the north to northwest flow in place.",
